#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""go-authored-batch3.py — authored sections for the remaining 6 topics.
Every example executed under go1.22.5 (sorted keys, comma-ok, range-copy trap,
remove/reverse/filter tricks, errors.Is through %w, errors.As custom type)."""

AUTHORED3 = {

"map-iteration-and-nil": [
  ("Is Go map iteration order random?",
   "Deliberately — the runtime randomizes the start so no code can rely on order. Sort the keys when order matters.",
   "It's random on purpose. The runtime picks a random starting bucket for each iteration, so the same map prints in different orders on different runs. Early Go had a deterministic order; people wrote code that depended on it; the Go team made it explicitly randomized so that dependency could never form silently. Now the spec says order is unspecified — treat every iteration order as an accident.\n\nWhen order matters, the pattern is fixed and small:\n\n```go\nkeys := make([]string, 0, len(m))\nfor k := range m {\n    keys = append(keys, k)\n}\nsort.Strings(keys)\nfor _, k := range keys {\n    fmt.Println(k, m[k])\n}\n```\n\nThe reason this matters in interviews and code review: code that 'worked' in testing with 3 keys can break in production with 300, because iteration order differences expose hidden assumptions — first-key-wins logic, dedup-by-iteration, pretty-printing diffs. If two runs of a test on the same map can disagree, the test is testing an accident.",
   "The randomness lives in the map iterator's initialization: a Go map is a hash table with buckets in a fixed layout, but the iterator selects its starting bucket and intra-bucket offset pseudo-randomly per iteration. Same map, same layout, different starting point — so the visit order differs every time.\n\nWhy the runtime pays for this deliberately: before randomization (pre-Go-1.0), map order was deterministic for a given insert sequence. Programs that only worked by that accident passed all their tests and shipped. The Go team flipped it to randomized so that any such dependency breaks loudly and immediately in development rather than quietly in production. It's a rare case of a runtime adding chaos as a correctness feature.\n\nThe sorted-keys pattern is the canonical alternative, and it's what the standard library's own tools do when they need determinism (text/template's sorted key iteration, flag's Visit using sorted lexicographic order). One allocation for the key slice, one sort, and the iteration order becomes a property of the data rather than of the hash seeds."),
  ("What is the comma-ok idiom for maps?",
   "v, ok := m[key] — ok is true only when the key exists. It distinguishes a stored zero value from a missing key.",
   "The two-value lookup answers presence and value separately:\n\n```go\nv, ok := m[key]\n```\n\nok is a bool: true means the key exists (and v holds its value — even if that value is the zero value), false means it doesn't (and v is the type's zero).\n\nThe reason it exists: the one-value lookup collapses 'missing' and 'stored zero' into the same result. m[\"admin\"] is false whether the key is absent or present-with-false — and those are usually very different situations. The comma-ok form splits them back apart.\n\n```go\n// ambiguous — rejects absent AND opted-out identically\nif !settings[\"notifications\"] { ... }\n\n// precise\nif enabled, ok := settings[\"notifications\"]; ok {\n    use(enabled)\n} else {\n    applyDefault()  // key never set\n}\n```\n\nUse it whenever a stored zero value is legitimate data — optional flags, counters at zero, empty strings that mean something. For accumulate patterns (m[w]++) the one-value form is correct because 'default to zero' is exactly what you want.",
   "Mechanically, the comma-ok lookup is the same hash-table probe as the one-value form; the runtime additionally reports whether the probe found a bucket entry. The mapaccess operations return both pieces; the single-value form just discards the boolean.\n\nThe idiom generalizes beyond maps — Go uses comma-ok wherever a bare value is ambiguous:\n\n- type assertions: `x, ok := i.(string)` — ok false instead of panic\n- channel receive: `v, ok := <-ch` — ok false means closed\n- function with ok: `strconv.Atoi`-style pairs\n\nIn every case, the pattern answers the same design question: 'the zero value of this type is also a valid in-band value, so success needs its own channel.' Learning comma-ok on maps is the entry point to a convention that spans the language — and recognizing when a bare value can't distinguish your cases is the skill.\n\nThe interview-ready framing: maps are total functions — every key gives an answer. When absence itself is information, you need the ok channel. When the default answer suffices, the one-value form keeps the code to what matters."),
  ("How do you delete from a map, and is it safe if the key is missing?",
   "delete(m, key) — safe on any key, including missing ones, and on nil maps. No error, no return value.",
   "Deletion is a one-way, no-feedback operation:\n\n```go\ndelete(m, \"stale-key\")\n```\n\nThere's no return value, no error, and no effect if the key doesn't exist. Deleting a missing key is a no-op, not a failure — the runtime probes for the entry and, finding none, does nothing. The same call works on a nil map without panic: probe nothing, do nothing.\n\nThat silence is the design question. Deletion being total (works on any input) means you never guard it — no 'if _, ok := m[k]; ok { delete(m, k) }' dance; just delete. But the flip side is you can't tell from the call whether anything happened. If you need 'was it there?', capture that with a lookup first:\n\n```go\nif _, ok := m[k]; ok {\n    delete(m, k)\n    metrics.Evictions++\n}\n```\n\nThe practical notes: deleting doesn't shrink the map's allocated capacity (memory isn't returned until the map is garbage), and deleting during range over the map is safe — the iteration contract allows deletion of not-yet-visited keys.",
   "Why is delete total while write panics on nil? The asymmetry has the same shape as the nil-map read rule: operations that need to 'find a place for something' require the structure to exist; operations that can honestly answer 'nothing there' don't.\n\nDelete is a lookup plus an unlink: probe for the key; if absent, there's nothing to unlink — done. No structure needed to express 'no effect.' Write, by contrast, needs a bucket to put the entry into — hence the panic on nil.\n\nThe iterate-while-deleting rule comes from the iterator's contract: entries deleted before the iterator reaches them may or may not be visited (unspecified); entries that exist and aren't deleted will be. That's enough to write cleanup loops safely:\n\n```go\nfor k, v := range cache {\n    if v.expired() {\n        delete(cache, k)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe pattern works because deletion can't invalidate the iteration — the iterator holds its position in the bucket structure, and delete unlinks entries without moving where subsequent iteration resumes. Insert during iteration is the unspecified case to avoid."),
  ("What operations are safe on a nil map?",
   "Reads (len, lookup → zero value), range (zero iterations), delete (no-op). Only writes panic.",
   "The full inventory, nil map on the left of each:\n\n```go\nvar m map[string]int\n\nlen(m)              // 0 — safe\ncap-ish size        // 0 — safe\nm[\"key\"]            // 0, false — the zero value, safe\nfor k := range m    // zero iterations — safe\ndelete(m, \"k\")      // no-op — safe\nm[\"k\"] = 1          // PANIC: assignment to entry in nil map\n```\n\nOne operation panics; everything else answers as if the map were empty. The rule mirrors nil-map semantics generally: the map value is a pointer to the hash structure; nil points at nothing; reads and deletes can honestly report 'nothing there,' but a write needs somewhere to put the entry.\n\nThe bug pattern this creates is partial: functions that only read tolerate a nil map fine, so the defect hides until some later change adds a write. The defensive styles, in order of preference:\n\n```go\nm := map[string]int{}    // literal empty — write-ready from birth\nm := make(map[string]int) // explicit\n```\n\nThe empty literal is one type-safe expression that kills the whole bug class — there is no state of a map you can't write to after `m := map[string]int{}`.",
   "The asymmetry is worth understanding rather than memorizing, because it's the same design decision appearing across Go. Consider the parallel with slices: a nil slice supports append (the allocate path creates storage on demand) — so 'var s []int; s = append(s, x)' is idiomatic. A nil map can't support 'insert on demand' for the same reason: creating a map's structure on demand would mean the write implicitly reassigns the map variable to a new pointer — but maps are passed by pointer already, and the runtime would have to reach into your variable to update it, which Go never does.\n\nSo the language's two collection types answer nil differently, each for a mechanical reason: the slice header is a value the runtime can return a new copy of (append's contract), while the map is a pointer whose replacement can only flow through assignment the caller performs. When people ask why Go is inconsistent here, that's the answer — it's not a philosophy difference, it's pointer-vs-value mechanics.\n\nFor API design, the consequence: functions that accept maps and might populate them should take-and-return (make inside, return the result) rather than fill-a-param — a nil map passed in can't be made writable from inside the function no matter what it does."),
  ("How do you iterate a map in sorted order?",
   "Collect keys into a slice, sort it, range over the sorted keys — the standard pattern, since map order is deliberately random.",
   "Since the language forbids relying on iteration order, deterministic iteration is a three-move pattern:\n\n```go\nkeys := make([]string, 0, len(m))  // pre-sized: no growth\nfor k := range m {\n    keys = append(keys, k)\n}\nsort.Strings(keys)\nfor _, k := range keys {\n    fmt.Println(k, m[k])\n}\n```\n\nExtract, sort, then walk the keys and index the map. The pre-size on the key slice matters at scale — len(m) is known, so the append loop never reallocates.\n\nThree variations you'll meet:\n\nsort.Strings for string keys, sort.Ints for ints; anything else gets sort.Slice(keys, func(i, j int) bool { ... }) with your comparison.\n\nIf you need value-ordered (top-N by count, say), sort the keys with a custom comparator that reads the map: sort.Slice(keys, func(i, j int) bool { return m[keys[i]] > m[keys[j]] }).\n\nIf you only need the first N sorted keys, sort a copy or use a partial approach — but profile before adding complexity; full sort of a key slice is fine at most real sizes.",
   "Why doesn't the language just offer a sorted map? Two answers: Go's map is a hash table with no order to expose (unlike a tree map), and the sorted-keys pattern composes with the sort package the language already has — the three-line idiom replaces a data structure.\n\nThe pattern's cost profile is worth knowing for performance-sensitive paths: O(n) key extraction, O(n log n) sort, then n map lookups (each O(1)). For iteration that must be deterministic at every scale, that's the honest price; the alternative (maintaining a sorted structure, like an ordered map or a B-tree keyed slice) trades O(log n) inserts for O(1) sorted walks — only worth it when sorted iteration dominates.\n\nThe standard library's own precedent: text/template sorts map keys for deterministic output — precisely so that template rendering doesn't change between runs. When your output is a diff, a hash, a golden-file test, or anything compared across executions, deterministic iteration isn't optional — the sorted-keys pattern is the fix. When it's just 'process every entry,' the random order is free and fine."),
],

"range-over-slice-map": [
  ("What does range over a slice give you, and what is the common trap?",
   "Index and a COPY of the element. Modifying the copy doesn't touch the slice — the trap: for _, v := range s { v.X = 1 } silently does nothing.",
   "The loop gives you two things per iteration: the position and a copy of the element at that position. The copy is the trap — anything you do to it stays done to the copy only.\n\n```go\ntype Task struct{ Done bool }\ntasks := []Task{{false}, {false}}\n\nfor _, t := range tasks {\n    t.Done = true      // modifies the COPY\n}\nfmt.Println(tasks[0].Done)  // false — nothing changed\n```\n\nThe fix is to use the index and write back through it:\n\n```go\nfor i := range tasks {\n    tasks[i].Done = true   // writes through to the slice's storage\n}\n```\n\nor range over a slice of pointers, where the copy is the pointer — pointing at the same underlying struct, so field writes land.\n\nThe `_` in the buggy version is where the tell lives: by discarding the index you lose the only write-back handle. `for i := range s` with `s[i]` is the pattern that can't fail this way — same simplicity, no copy hazard."),
  ("Can you modify a map's values while ranging over it?",
   "Yes — updating existing keys during range is allowed. Adding new keys during iteration is unspecified; deleting is safe.",
   "Three operations, three rules. Update existing values: safe and common — the iteration visits each key once, and writing to a key it has or hasn't reached yet is well-defined.\n\n```go\nfor k, v := range m {\n    m[k] = v * 2     // safe — updating existing entries\n}\n```\n\nDelete during range: safe — entries deleted before the iterator reaches them may or may not be visited (unspecified, but not a crash), and deletion never invalidates iteration:\n\n```go\nfor k := range m {\n    if expired(k) {\n        delete(m, k)   // safe\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAdd new keys: unspecified — the iterator may or may not visit entries added during the loop, and it's easy to construct an infinite loop (adding while iterating reaching the new entries, adding again). The GC-safe rule everyone uses: don't insert during range; if you must, collect the new keys first and insert after the loop.\n\nThe mental model: range takes a snapshot of the iteration mechanics, not the contents. Mutation of values stays consistent; structural changes (delete) are tolerated with relaxed promises; growth (insert) is where the runtime refuses to promise anything."),
  ("Does range copy the slice?",
   "range evaluates the slice expression once — copying the header. Length is fixed at loop start: appends inside the loop are invisible to it.",
   "The range expression is evaluated exactly once, at loop start. For a slice, that means the header — pointer, length, capacity — is copied once into the loop's internal state. The elements are not copied; the loop reads through the pointer.\n\nThe observable consequence: the loop's length is frozen at entry. Appends inside the body grow a different (new) backing array — the loop keeps walking the original header's extent:\n\n```go\ns := []int{1, 2, 3}\nfor i, v := range s {\n    s = append(s, v*10)      // grows s — the loop doesn't see it\n    fmt.Println(i, v)\n}\nfmt.Println(len(s))  // 6 — the appends DID land, after the loop's snapshot\n```\n\nThree iterations, not six — the loop captured len 3 at start. This is why range-over-slice can't infinite-loop on self-append the way some for-loops in other languages can.\n\nThe same once-only rule explains the nested pattern: ranging s and ranging s[i] inside — each inner range snapshots its own expression, and neither is affected by outer-body changes to s's length. It's consistent: one evaluation, one snapshot, every range."),
  ("What is the classic range gotcha with loop variables (pre Go 1.22)?",
   "Before 1.22, the loop variable was ONE reused variable — closures and goroutines capturing it saw the last value. 1.22 gives each iteration a fresh variable.",
   "The bug every Go codebase of a certain age contains:\n\n```go\n// Go 1.21 and earlier\nfor _, task := range tasks {\n    go func() {\n        process(task)   // captures the SHARED task variable\n    }()\n}\n// all goroutines likely process the LAST task — by the time they run,\n// task has been reassigned to the final iteration's value\n```\n\nThe cause: pre-1.22, the loop variable was a single memory location reused across iterations. The closure captured that location, not the value — so when goroutines finally executed, they all read whatever the loop left in it: the last value.\n\nThe old fixes were workarounds: pass it in (`go func(t Task) { process(t) }(task)`) or shadow it (`task := task` inside the loop).\n\nGo 1.22 changed the semantics: each iteration gets a fresh variable. The closure-capture bug simply disappears on 1.22+ — capturing the loop variable now captures that iteration's value. This is a language change, not a runtime optimization, so the answer must be version-scoped: on 1.22+, the gotcha is history; on older code you still meet it, and the pass-it-in fix is still the right pattern for clarity.",
   "Why did the old semantics exist at all? Performance — one variable, one address, reused: the loop didn't allocate per iteration. The cost was that the single-address decision leaked into closures, and closures capture by reference in Go. The two features composed into the most common Go bug of the 2010s.\n\nThe 1.22 fix is a rare breaking-ish change the Go team could make because the old behavior was almost never intended — the proposals (years of them) documented that the capture bug dominated every legitimate use of the shared variable. The compatibility promise bent to reality: code relying on the old behavior was almost certainly broken code.\n\nFor interviews and code review today: mention the version. 'Each iteration gets a fresh variable since 1.22' is the current truth; 'capture the loop variable in a goroutine and you'll see the last value' is the historical truth that explains why old code is full of pass-through parameters and why linters still flag the pattern. Both halves are knowledge; the version boundary is where they meet."),
  ("How do you skip or break in a range loop?",
   "continue skips to the next iteration; break exits the loop. Labels break out of NESTED loops — Go has no labeled continue.",
   "The two keywords cover the single-loop cases:\n\n```go\nfor _, v := range items {\n    if skip(v) {\n        continue        // next iteration\n    }\n    if fatal(v) {\n        break           // exit the loop entirely\n    }\n    process(v)\n}\n```\n\nThe case the keywords don't cover: breaking out of a NESTED loop from the inner body. That's what labels are for — the one labeled-statement use that survives modern Go:\n\n```go\nouter:\n    for _, row := range matrix {\n        for _, cell := range row {\n            if cell == target {\n                break outer    // exits BOTH loops\n            }\n        }\n    }\n```\n\nNote what Go deliberately lacks: labeled continue. goto exists (and is occasionally the clean answer for error-cleanup ladders), but the loop-control set is continue, break, and labeled break — no labeled continue, which the designers considered unnecessary after the labeled-break case.\n\nIn idiomatic Go, deep nesting with labels is rare — the culture prefers early returns and small functions, so a labeled break usually signals a loop body that wants to be its own function returning an error/flag. Labels are the honest tool when extraction would be more ceremony; extraction is the default when it reads better."),
],

"slice-tricks": [
  ("How do you remove an element from a slice in Go?",
   "Order preserved: s = append(s[:i], s[i+1:]...) — shifts the tail left. Order destroyed: s[i] = s[len(s)-1]; s = s[:len(s)-1] — O(1) swap-delete.",
   "Two idioms, two contracts. The append-shift preserves order and costs O(n-i) — everything after the removed element copies one slot left:\n\n```go\ns := []int{1, 2, 3, 4}\ns = append(s[:1], s[2:]...)   // remove index 1\n// s is now [1 3 4]\n```\n\nThe mechanics: s[2:] is the tail [3 4]; append copies it onto s starting at index 1; the result's header is reassigned to s. The elements move — that's the O(n) price of keeping order.\n\nThe swap-delete trades order for O(1) — copy the last element into the hole, shrink by one:\n\n```go\ns := []int{1, 2, 3, 4}\ns[1] = s[len(s)-1]      // [1 4 3 4]\ns = s[:len(s)-1]        // [1 4 3]\n```\n\nNo shifting, no copy loop — one store and one reslice. This is the standard trick for removing from unordered collections (task queues, membership sets, draw-pools) where position means nothing. The interview answer that stands out is knowing BOTH and naming the invariant that decides: is order part of this collection's meaning?"),
  ("How do you insert an element at a position in a slice?",
   "s = append(s[:i], append([]T{x}, s[i:]...)...) — or the manual grow-shift for zero extra allocation.",
   "The one-liner composes two appends: build a temp slice holding the new element plus the tail, then overwrite the shifted region:\n\n```go\ns := []int{1, 2, 3, 4}\ni := 2\ns = append(s[:i], append([]int{99}, s[i:]...)...)\n// s is [1 2 99 3 4]\n```\n\nIt reads inside-out: s[i:] is [3 4]; append([]int{99}, that...) is [99 3 4]; appending that onto s[:2] ([1 2]) gives [1 2 99 3 4]. Correct, and the standard reference form — but it allocates a temp slice for the tail, so the cost is a copy plus garbage.\n\nThe allocation-free version does what the runtime would: grow once, shift the tail right by one, store the element:\n\n```go\ns = append(s, 0)             // grow by one (zero value at end)\ncopy(s[i+1:], s[i:])         // shift tail right\ns[i] = 99                   // place the element\n```\n\ncopy handles the overlapping move correctly (it's defined for overlap). Same result, one allocation, no temp. When insertion is in a hot path, the three-step version is the one to write; the one-liner is the one to remember."),
  ("How do you copy a slice safely in Go?",
   "make+copy: dst := make([]T, len(s)); copy(dst, s) — or append([]T(nil), s...). Both give new backing storage; t := s shares.",
   "The safe copies are the two that guarantee new backing storage:\n\n```go\n// explicit — capacity known\ndst := make([]int, len(s))\ncopy(dst, s)\n\n// idiom — one line, append picks capacity\nv := append([]int(nil), s...)\n```\n\nBoth leave you with an independent slice: writes to dst don't appear in s and vice versa. The test is exactly that:\n\n```go\ndst[0] = 50\nfmt.Println(s[0])   // original value — untouched\n```\n\nWhat is NOT a copy: `t := s`. That shares the header — same backing array — which is the aliasing behavior, not the isolation behavior. (Verified: after t[0] = 100 through a plain assignment, s[0] reads 100.)\n\nThe boundary case that bites in practice: a slice of POINTERS. copy and the append-idiom both copy the pointer values — new headers, same pointees. *dst[0] = x still reaches the object s[0] points at. For pointer elements, slice-level copying is shallow by construction; deep isolation requires copying the pointed-to values, which is your code, not the language's."),
  ("How do you reverse a slice?",
   "Two-pointer swap: walk inward from both ends, swapping — in place, no allocation.",
   "The canonical loop:\n\n```go\nfor i, j := 0, len(s)-1; i < j; i, j = i+1, j-1 {\n    s[i], s[j] = s[j], s[i]\n}\n```\n\nTwo indexes — one from each end — swap, walk toward the middle, stop when they cross. In place (no new slice), O(n) time, O(1) space, and len/2 swaps total.\n\nThings worth knowing around it:\n\nThe condition is i < j, not i <= j — the middle element (odd lengths) doesn't need to swap with itself.\n\nIt mutates the input — when the caller needs the original too, copy first (previous trick), then reverse the copy.\n\nThere's no standard-library Reverse (a deliberate omission; the loop is 3 lines and adding it would invite the same for dozens of other micro-operations). Go 1.21+'s slices package has a Reverse — worth mentioning as the modern one-call answer:\n\n```go\nimport \"slices\"\nslices.Reverse(s)   // same two-pointer, library-maintained\n```"),
  ("How do you filter a slice into a new one?",
   "Append matches onto a fresh slice — filtered := make([]T, 0); for ... append. Or the in-place filter: out := s[:0] — reuses storage but overwrites the source.",
   "The straightforward form allocates a fresh destination:\n\n```go\nfiltered := make([]int, 0)\nfor _, v := range all {\n    if v%2 == 0 {\n        filtered = append(filtered, v)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nNew storage, source untouched — the safe default.\n\nThe in-place form starts the output AT the source's first slot and overwrites forward:\n\n```go\nevens := all[:0]\nfor _, v := range all {\n    if v%2 == 0 {\n        evens = append(evens, v)\n    }\n}\n// all is now CLOBBERED — verified: [2 4 3 4 5] after filtering [1 2 3 4 5]\n```\n\nZero allocations — the kept values are written into the same backing array the loop is reading from, always at or behind the read position, so no unread value is lost. The price is explicit: the source slice's contents are destroyed (its length may still say 5, but the tail is garbage). This is the standard idiom inside libraries where the input is a scratch copy; it's a bug in application code that keeps using the input afterward.\n\nThe interview-useful framing: know both, and treat the in-place filter as an optimization with a visible precondition — 'the caller no longer needs the input' — that should be stated at the function boundary, not discovered by a reader at 2 a.m."),
],

"if-err-not-nil-pattern": [
  ("Why is if err != nil the standard Go error check?",
   "Errors are values: functions return (result, error); the caller checks immediately at the point of failure. The if err != nil block IS Go's exception handling.",
   "Go made a language-design bet: failures should be visible in the code, not hidden in a throw path. So a Go function that can fail says so in its return values, and the caller's first order of business is checking:\n\n```go\ndata, err := os.ReadFile(path)\nif err != nil {\n    return fmt.Errorf(\"load config: %w\", err)\n}\n// data is valid here — no ambiguity\n```\n\nThat guard is the entire error-handling mechanism. There's no catch block, no finally, no exception hierarchy to know — just a value check where the failure occurred. The pattern repeats at every layer, and each layer chooses: return it up (wrapped with context), handle it (retry, default, log), or crash (genuinely unrecoverable).\n\nWhat the pattern buys is auditability: a reader can see every failure path of a function by reading the function — no stack of handlers elsewhere, no documentation of what throws. The cost is the visible ceremony — Go code has more if err != nil lines than exception code has catch keywords, and that verbosity is the most-criticized and most-defended property of the language.\n\nThe deeper point for an interview: this isn't boilerplate to minimize; it's control flow made literal. Every line of error handling is a decision the author made at a specific failure point — which is exactly what code review wants to see."),
  ("Should you check err after checking the result?",
   "Check err first. On failure, other returns are zero values — reading them before the check is the classic bug.",
   "Check the error before anything else. The convention — on failure, other return values are zero/nil — means reading the result before the check reads garbage:\n\n```go\nn, err := reader.Read(buf)\nprocess(buf[:n])   // BUG on error: n is 0, or worse, stale\nif err != nil {\n    return err\n}\n```\n\nThe order matters because the zero-value convention makes the result slot meaningless when err is non-nil. buf[:0] processes nothing — silently, no error — or with a partially-filled buffer from a previous call, processes stale data. Either way the bug is invisible until you trace why 'nothing happened.'\n\nThe guard-first shape:\n\n```go\nn, err := reader.Read(buf)\nif err != nil {\n    return err\n}\nprocess(buf[:n])   // n is valid ONLY below the guard\n```\n\nThe one documented exception: io.Reader's Read returns the count of bytes read before an error (including io.EOF) — data read before the failure is real, and callers should use it. That exception is famous BECAUSE it's the exception: everywhere else, the rule is absolute — err first, values after, never reversed."),
  ("What is the difference between returning err and wrapping it?",
   "Returning err passes it up unchanged; fmt.Errorf with %w adds context while keeping errors.Is/As working. Wrap when you add meaning; pass through when you add nothing.",
   "Pass-through and wrap are both returns, with different information outcomes:\n\n```go\n// pass-through: caller sees the original error, unchanged\nif err != nil {\n    return err\n}\n\n// wrap: the caller sees what YOU were doing when it failed\nif err != nil {\n    return fmt.Errorf(\"load config %s: %w\", path, err)\n}\n```\n\nThe pass-through is honest when your function adds nothing: a thin delegation where the caller already knows the operation. Wrapping is the default whenever your layer knows something the error doesn't carry — which file, which step, which request — because the caller's debugging question is always 'doing WHAT when this failed?'\n\nThe %w is what makes wrapping safe: it preserves the causal chain, so errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) still works through your added text. %v would embed the message but cut the chain — the caller could no longer match the underlying cause.\n\nThe anti-pattern to name: wrapping with no information. fmt.Errorf(\"failed: %w\", err) adds five characters and zero meaning — every layer doing it stacks 'failed: failed: failed:' around the real message. Wrap when there's something to say; pass through when there isn't."),
  ("What is the blank identifier's role with errors?",
   "_, err := f() keeps only the error. v, _ := f() discards the error — a lint smell unless failure is genuinely impossible. _ makes ignoring an explicit choice.",
   "The blank identifier is Go's escape valve for unused returns, and errors are where it matters most:\n\n```go\n_, err := f()    // need only the error — fine, common\nv, _ := f()      // take the value, ignore the error — the dangerous one\n```\n\n`v, _` is legal Go and sometimes correct — best-effort writes, metrics, caches where failure changes nothing. The _ documents the decision at the call site: a reader sees the error was considered and dropped, not overlooked.\n\nBut most `v, _` in real code is a latent bug. The failure doesn't vanish — it moves downstream. The zero value gets used, and the cause becomes 'why is this empty?' three weeks later. No compile error, no test failure — the error was already gone at the point where it could have been handled.\n\nThat's why errcheck exists as a linter: the language permits discarding, the ecosystem flags it. The alternatives in order of effort: log it (`v, err := f(); if err != nil { log... }` — three lines, failure becomes diagnosable), handle it, or propagate it. Reach for `_` only when you can say why the error doesn't matter — out loud, at that line."),
  ("Why does Go not use try/catch?",
   "Visible failure paths: signatures document what can fail, callers deal at the call site, and the compiler enforces acknowledgment. Panic is reserved for program bugs.",
   "Go's designers looked at exception systems and saw a cost they didn't want: invisible control flow. Any call in an exception language might throw — the failure path isn't in the code, it's in the documentation. Go made the opposite trade: failure is a value in the signature, and the caller must acknowledge it before using the result.\n\nWhat that buys:\n\nAuditable failure — reading a function shows its failure handling; there is no elsewhere to check.\n\nForced acknowledgment — unused-variable rules mean you can't silently skip the error; discarding it requires writing _, which is a visible choice.\n\nLocal reasoning — the failure and its handling are adjacent, in the same function, at the same call. No unwinding through five frames to find the handler.\n\nWhat it costs: the ceremony is real. Three lines per fallible call, in every layer, forever. The Go community's own essays acknowledge this — the verbosity is the price of the visibility, and the bet is that visibility wins at maintenance time.\n\nThe design keeps panic/recover for the other kind of failure — the program is wrong, not the input — which is why the two mechanisms don't overlap: try/catch conflates expected failure with bugs; Go splits them at the language level."),
],

"fmt-errorf": [
  ("What does fmt.Errorf with %w do?",
   "Creates an error that wraps another error — adding your message while keeping errors.Is/As working through the chain.",
   "%w is the wrap verb. It builds a new error whose message is your formatted text with the wrapped error's message embedded, AND records the wrapped error as unwrap-able:\n\n```go\nbase := errors.New(\"disk full\")\nwrapped := fmt.Errorf(\"write config: %w\", base)\n\n// prints: write config: disk full\n// AND: errors.Is(wrapped, base) == true — the chain is intact\n```\n\nThat second property is the whole point. Any caller can ask 'was the underlying cause disk full?' and get the right answer, no matter how many layers wrapped it. Without %w — with %v — the message would print the same, but the causal link would be severed: the chain is data the error carries, not text.",
   "Mechanically, %w tells fmt.Errorf to store the wrapped error in the returned error's internal tree (an fmt.wrapError holding both your format result and the cause). The errors package's Is and As walk that tree via Unwrap: each %w is an edge, and Is/As traverse edges until they find a match or exhaust the chain.\n\nThe walk semantics are the interview-grade detail:\n\n- errors.Is(e, target) — walks the chain, comparing each node to target (identity or the target's Is method). True if the cause is anywhere below.\n- errors.As(e, &typedTarget) — walks until it finds an error assignable to the type, then copies it into your target.\n- Both treat the chain as read-only evidence: they never unwrap past a %v (there's no edge to follow).\n\nThe composed behavior — wrap at every layer with %w, inspect with Is/As at the boundary — is Go's answer to exception type-matching, but with the chain explicit in the data rather than in a type hierarchy."),
  ("%w vs %v in error messages — what is the practical difference?",
   "They print identically. %w preserves the unwrap chain (errors.Is/As work); %v bakes the text in and cuts the chain.",
   "The two lines look the same and behave differently:\n\n```go\nbase := errors.New(\"disk full\")\nwithW := fmt.Errorf(\"write config: %w\", base)\nwithV := fmt.Errorf(\"write config: %v\", base)\n\n// both print: \"write config: disk full\"\n\nerrors.Is(withW, base)   // true\nerrors.Is(withV, base)   // false — the chain is gone\n```\n\n%w stores the cause as a linkable edge; %v renders the cause's message into the string at construction and keeps no reference. The information content of the message is identical — the difference is entirely whether the machine can still ask questions about the cause.\n\nSo the rule: %w whenever a caller below you might need to match or extract the cause (which is most wrapping — you rarely know your caller's needs). %v when the error is headed for a human log line and no code will inspect it — a top-level 'failed to start: %v' that nothing branches on.\n\nThe bug shape to recognize: someone 'cleaned up' %w to %v for style, and six months later a caller's errors.Is quietly became always-false. The test that would catch it is a test on the wrap — which most codebases never write."),
  ("Can you wrap multiple errors with fmt.Errorf?",
   "Not with %w — exactly one. Go 1.20's errors.Join(err1, err2) wraps several into one error that Is-matches each component.",
   "fmt.Errorf's %w takes one cause — the format can contain it once, and the wrap tree holds one edge. To wrap several failures, Go 1.20 added errors.Join:\n\n```go\nvar errs []error\nfor _, task := range tasks {\n    if err := task.Run(); err != nil {\n        errs = append(errs, fmt.Errorf(\"task %s: %w\", task.Name, err))\n    }\n}\nreturn errors.Join(errs...)   // one error, every failure\n```\n\nThe joined error prints all components' messages (newline-joined), and errors.Is/As walk every component — any cause anywhere in the batch is findable:\n\nerrors.Is(joined, someComponentErr) is true for each. errors.As extracts any component's type.\n\nThe pattern it serves: parallel validation or batch execution where returning the first error throws away information. 'Form had 3 invalid fields' beats 'form had 1 invalid field' three round-trips in a row. Sequential code still returns the first error — the later steps didn't run, so reporting them would be fiction."),
  ("When should you add context with Errorf instead of returning err directly?",
   "When your layer adds meaning the caller can't infer: the operation, the file, the request. If the error already says everything, pass it through.",
   "The judgment call in every error return. The test I apply: does my layer know something the error doesn't carry?\n\n```go\n// the error says 'no such file' — my layer knows WHICH file\nif err != nil {\n    return fmt.Errorf(\"read config %s: %w\", path, err)\n}\n\n// the error says everything; my function is a thin pass-through\nif err != nil {\n    return err\n}\n```\n\nThe debugging reality drives it: the caller's question is always 'doing what, to which thing, when this failed?' os.ErrNotExist means one thing everywhere; your function knows it was the user's config, the payment retry, the migration step. Wrapping attaches that knowledge where the failure happened — the only place it's known accurately.\n\nThe counter-balance: every wrap adds reading. A stack of `fmt.Errorf(\"failed: %w\", ...)` through five layers produces 'failed: failed: failed: disk full' — noise, no signal. Wrap when you have something to say, pass when you don't. The %w stays either way — a chain of meaningful wraps composes into a readable narrative: 'start server: load config: open /etc/app.conf: no such file or directory.'"),
  ("What does errors.Is do with a wrapped chain?",
   "Walks the Unwrap chain comparing each error to the target. fmt.Errorf %w links the chain; Is finds the sentinel anywhere inside.",
   "errors.Is is the chain-aware equality check:\n\n```go\nvar ErrNotFound = errors.New(\"not found\")\n\nerr := fmt.Errorf(\"get user: %w\", ErrNotFound)\n\nerrors.Is(err, ErrNotFound)   // true — found through the wrap\nerr == ErrNotFound            // false — they're different values\n```\n\nThe walk: Is takes the error and follows Unwrap edges — each %w is one edge — comparing each node against the target with == (or the target's Is method, if it has one). The sentinel can sit any number of wraps deep; Is finds it because the chain is traversable data.\n\nThis is why Is replaced raw == for sentinels: == sees only the top value (the outermost wrap — not equal), while Is sees the whole causal history. A caller that uses == breaks the moment any intermediate layer wraps; a caller that uses Is survives arbitrary wrapping — which is exactly the compat contract between layers that don't know each other.\n\nThe As variant does the same walk for types: errors.As(err, &myErrTypedPointer) finds the first node assignable to the type and copies it out. Is for identities, As for types with fields — both powered by the same chain."),
],

"custom-error-types-basics": [
  ("How do you create a custom error type in Go?",
   "Any type implementing error: a struct plus an Error() string method. Callers extract it with errors.As.",
   "The error interface is one method. A struct that adds it becomes an error, and its fields travel with the failure:\n\n```go\ntype NotFoundError struct {\n    Name string\n}\n\nfunc (e *NotFoundError) Error() string {\n    return \"not found: \" + e.Name\n}\n\n// returning it\nfunc lookup(name string) error {\n    return &NotFoundError{Name: name}\n}\n```\n\nThe caller's side is errors.As — the type-aware chain walk:\n\n```go\nerr := lookup(\"cfg\")\nvar nfe *NotFoundError\nif errors.As(err, &nfe) {\n    fmt.Println(\"which one:\", nfe.Name)   // the field the caller needed\n}\n```\n\nThat's the whole pattern: struct for the data, Error() for the message, pointer receiver for the method, errors.As at the consumer. The value over a plain errors.New string is exactly the fields — 'which record failed' stops being prose in a message and becomes data the caller branches on."),
  ("How do callers inspect a custom error?",
   "var target *MyError; errors.As(err, &target) — walks the wrap chain, finds the first error of that type, copies it into target.",
   "errors.As is the extraction tool:\n\n```go\nvar rateLimited *RateLimitError\nif errors.As(err, &rateLimited) {\n    time.Sleep(rateLimited.RetryAfter)\n    retry()\n}\n```\n\nThe pattern always has the same shape: declare a target of the error's type (pointer, because methods hang on pointers), pass its address to As, and As fills it if the chain contains a match. The wrap chain is traversed the same way Is traverses it — so intermediate layers wrapping with %w don't hide your type from the caller.\n\nThe pointer-vs-value detail matters: if your Error() method has a pointer receiver, the error stored in the chain is a *NotFoundError, so the target must be *NotFoundError — var target NotFoundError with &target makes As look for a value type and never match. This mismatch (value target for a pointer error) is the most common As bug, and it compiles cleanly — it just returns false forever.\n\nAs answers 'does this failure carry typed data, and give me that data.' Is answers 'is this failure this specific sentinel.' Together they cover the two reasons a caller inspects errors at all."),
  ("Why define error types instead of using errors.New strings?",
   "Typed errors carry data and match by type; string errors match by identity only. Types make the failure actionable.",
   "The sentinel (errors.New) gives the caller one bit: is it this error? The type gives the caller the error AND its specifics:\n\n```go\n// sentinel — the caller learns WHICH failure, nothing else\nvar ErrRateLimited = errors.New(\"rate limited\")\n\n// type — the caller learns which failure and HOW LONG to wait\ntype RateLimitError struct{ RetryAfter time.Duration }\n```\n\nThe caller's next action is the whole point. With the sentinel, the caller can branch — 'if rate limited, use the backup' — but not act on specifics. With the type, the caller reads the fields and does something with them: sleep the RetryAfter, show the UserMessage, redirect to the Location, log the Which-Record.\n\nWhen types become mandatory: the moment the caller's correct behavior depends on data about the failure, not just the category. Retry logic needs a duration. HTTP error responses need a status code. Partial-failure reporting needs which shard. String errors can't carry any of this — parsing the message is the forbidden alternative, because it couples the caller to your prose.\n\nThe progression in real codebases: start with errors.New sentinels, promote to a custom type when a caller first needs to ask a question the message can't answer structurally. The standard library made the same journey — os.PathError exists because 'which path?' is the first question every file-error caller asks."),
  ("Should custom errors be pointer or value receivers?",
   "Pointer receivers, and errors.As with a pointer target — the ecosystem convention. Mixing value and pointer error types is the classic As-mismatch bug.",
   "Convention: pointer. Declare the type's Error() on the pointer, return &MyError{...}, and match with a pointer target in errors.As:\n\n```go\nfunc (e *NotFoundError) Error() string { ... }   // pointer receiver\nreturn &NotFoundError{Name: name}               // pointer value\n\nvar target *NotFoundError                       // pointer target\nerrors.As(err, &target)\n```\n\nThe receiver and the target must agree: As matches the dynamic type in the chain against the target's type. Pointer error, pointer target — match. Value error (Error() on the value receiver, stored as a value), value target — match. Mixing them is the silent bug: a pointer error against a value target compiles and always returns false, because a *NotFoundError is not assignable to NotFoundError.\n\nWhy pointer won as the convention: pointer receivers are Go's default for anything with identity semantics, they keep large error structs cheap to pass, they allow nil-ness (var x *MyError is nil before assignment), and one rule everywhere beats two rules matched by discipline. The standard library's typed errors (os.PathError, net.AddrError, etc.) are pointer-style. When in doubt, look at what the ecosystem does and do that — error code is read by strangers."),
  ("What is the error interface exactly?",
   "One method: Error() string. Everything else — wrapping, Is, As, Unwrap — is the errors package's machinery built on top.",
   "The interface that everything else stands on:\n\n```go\ntype error interface {\n    Error() string\n}\n```\n\nAny type with that method IS an error — no registration, no inheritance, no base class. errors.New's *errorString, fmt.Errorf's wrappers, your structs, third-party types: all just implement Error().\n\nThe important separation: the interface itself carries nothing but the message. Wrapping (%w), matching (errors.Is), type extraction (errors.As), chain traversal (Unwrap) are all behaviors of the errors package operating on errors that choose to participate — an error implements Unwrap() to join the wrapping machinery, Is(error) bool to customize matching, and so on. A bare struct with only Error() is a fully valid error; it just can't be wrapped-into or inspected beyond its message.\n\nThat minimalism is the design: one method makes errors universal (anything can fail with anything), and the optional protocol methods (Unwrap, Is, Unwrap []error) layer on capabilities without bloating the base contract. When you understand that the interface is just Error() and the errors package is a protocol on top, the whole error system stops being magic — it's interfaces all the way down, which is Go's answer to most questions."),
],
}
